  "domainCertificates": {
    "bessel": "The entire defining series covers every displayed order. For 0<z<=2 its normalized alternating series is strictly positive, including nu=0,z=2 by the next paired terms; hence alpha>2. Jnu and Jnu+1 cannot share a positive zero: the derivative recurrence would give zero value and derivative at a regular ODE point, contradicting its nonzero series solution. Thus B=Jnu+1(alpha)^2>0. Each pair difference or sum is B times a rational polynomial of u=alpha^2-4>0 with coefficients of one strict sign.",
    "green": "All a are positive integers. sinh(a)>0, so all displayed denominators are nonzero. Selected-answer equality is exact. Every difference and sum has a rational enclosure obtained from exponential series and exact interval arithmetic."
  },
  "optionsVisibleDifficulty": {
    "bessel": "At least one positive root-independent boundary-factor distractor B or B/4 accompanies B/2. Positivity and alpha>2 therefore leave multiple candidates. Identifying the half factor requires the boundary differential identity and adjacent-order derivative recurrence; this is an editorial assessment, pending independent reviewer judgment.",
    "green": "The singleton opposite magnitude clue is removed. Positive-integral information is legitimate but no longer isolates the known paired magnitude. Constructing and differentiating the Green resolvent trace remains the intended decision."
